\begin_layout Subsection*
Problem:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You have a point observer that sees at a set of points along a unit sphere.
 At each of these points the observer measures the optic flow,
 but does not have access to the depth of the object.
 In this world objects can be at any depth,
 but they do not move.
 All motion is generated from self-motion of the observer.
 How can we go from this set of optic flow to the rotational and translational speed of the observer?
 To solve this,
 we will define the equation for how optic flow is generated based on an observer's self-motion and then invert it to get the self-motion from the optic flow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Optic flow equation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}=-\mathbf{\Omega}\times\mathbf{x}_{i}+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T},\quad i=1,...,N$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th sample in visual space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x\mathbf{}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $||\mathbf{x}_{i}||=1$
\end_inset

:
 viewing direction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 is measured optic flow tangent to the viewing direction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

:
 unknown angular velocity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

:
 unknown translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}_{i}>0$
\end_inset

:
 unknown depth along 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}:=I-\mathbf{x}_{i}\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\intercal}$
\end_inset

:
 projector onto the tangent plane at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pull out cross product into a matrix equation.
 For a vector 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{a}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
a_{x}\\
a_{y}\\
a_{z}
\end{array}\right],\quad[\mathbf{a}]_{\times}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
0 & -a_{z} & a_{y}\\
a_{z} & 0 & -a_{x}\\
-a_{y} & a_{x} & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Omega^{*}=(A^{\intercal}A)^{-1}A^{\intercal}\mathbf{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T^{*}=\sigma_{min}^{right}(C)\text{RMS}(v)
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{min}^{right}$
\end_inset

 is admittedly poor notation which means the right hand singular vector with the smallest sigma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z_{i}=\frac{||w_{i}||^{2}}{v_{i}\cdot w_{i}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To improve the approximations,
 numerically minimize
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\Omega},\hat{T},\{\hat{Z}_{i}\}=\arg\min_{\Omega,T,\{Z_{i}\}}\sum_{i}||u_{i}+\Omega\times x_{i}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}T||^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\left[\begin{array}{c}
[x_{1}]_{\times}\\{}
[x_{2}]_{\times}\\
\vdots\\{}
[x_{N}]_{\times}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Omega^{*}=(A^{\intercal}A)^{-1}A^{\intercal}\mathbf{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{v}_{i}:=\mathbf{u}_{i}-[\mathbf{x}_{i}]_{\times}\Omega^{*}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C:=\left[\begin{array}{c}
[\mathbf{v}_{1}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{1}\\{}
[\mathbf{v}_{2}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{2}\\
\vdots\\{}
[\mathbf{v}_{N}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{N}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{i}=\Pi_{i}T^{*}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Derivation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}=[\mathbf{a}]_{\times}\mathbf{b}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{a}=-[\mathbf{a}]_{\times}\mathbf{b}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\mathbf{\Omega}\times\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\mathbf{x}_{i}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Omega}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=[\mathbf{x}_{i}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Omega}+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 estimate rotational motion by ignoring translation.
 I believe this is valid with dense and symmetric coverage of a sphere.
 Need to check
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}\approx[\mathbf{x}_{i}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Omega}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stack the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 observations and the view points to make a linear equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\left[\begin{array}{c}
[x_{1}]_{\times}\\{}
[x_{2}]_{\times}\\
\vdots\\{}
[x_{N}]_{\times}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\mathbf{u}_{1}\\
\mathbf{u}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{u}_{N}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\mathbf{\Omega}}=(A^{\intercal}A)^{-1}A^{\intercal}\mathbf{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subtract our estimate of rotation and solve for translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{v}_{i}:=\mathbf{u}_{i}-[\mathbf{x}_{i}]_{\times}\hat{\mathbf{\Omega}}\approx\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is colinear with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\Pi}_{i}T$
\end_inset

 with constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{Z_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
 So
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}\times(\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T})=\mathbf{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[\mathbf{v}_{i}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{i}\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C:=\left[\begin{array}{c}
[\mathbf{v}_{1}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{1}\\{}
[\mathbf{v}_{2}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{2}\\
\vdots\\{}
[\mathbf{v}_{N}]_{\times}\mathbf{\Pi}_{N}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CT=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since this is homogenous linear it doesn't have a unique solution,
 so find a 
\series bold
T
\series default
 subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $||\mathbf{T}||=1$
\end_inset

.
 
\series bold
T
\series default
 is the right singular vector of 
\series bold
C
\series default
 corresponding to the smallest singular value.
 The singular value itself just gives the direction of motion,
 but you can give it a scale by multiplying the vector by RMS(v) which makes it proportional to T/Z
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recover relative depths 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}=\frac{1}{Z_{i}}w_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z_{i}=\frac{||w_{i}||^{2}}{v_{i}\cdot w_{i}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then refine to iron out the approximation step with a nonlinear optimizer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega},\hat{T},\{\hat{Z}_{i}\}=\sum_{i}||u_{i}+\Omega\times x_{i}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}T||^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

